    "answer": {
      "short": "$r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}}$.\n\nUse $x=r^2$ to solve $x^2-x-1=0$, then take the positive square root.",
      "worked": "Since $r>1$, the longest side is $r^2$, so it is the hypotenuse.\n$$\\begin{align*}1^2+r^2&=(r^2)^2\\\\1+r^2&=r^4\\end{align*}$$\nLet $x=r^2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1+x&=x^2\\\\x^2-x-1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt{(-1)^2-4(1)(-1)}}{2}\\\\x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x=r^2>1$, reject the negative root.\n$$\\begin{align*}r^2&=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\\\r&=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
